<h1>what do i do if my school has no recruiting from companies i want</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">your school's career fair is a filter, not a path. the companies you want probably never show up, and the ones that do are fishing for brand names, not talent. the fix is building proof they can find anywhere and putting it where recruiters already search.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>new-grad hiring at big tech is down more than 50% from 2019, and new grads are only about <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">7% of hires</a>, the pipeline was already narrow, and your school not being on the list makes it invisible</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers now use skills-based hiring</a>, up from 81% the year before, which means your gpa and campus matter less than what you can actually do</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">63.1% of internships convert to full-time</a> for 2024-25, but most non-target students never hear about them because postings live inside closed campus networks</li>
    <li>an internship cuts your odds of underemployment by <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">48.5%</a>, yet students at non-target schools report finding them through cold outreach, not career centers</li>
    <li>the real gap is not company snobbery, it is proof architecture: most students have no scored, discoverable work, so recruiters default to the schools they already know</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>why the campus recruiting game is rigged against you</h2>
  
  <p>universities run on a time-based filter operating system: you progress by waiting years and not failing. career services is an extension of that filter. the companies that show up at your career fair are not there to find the best talent. they are there to efficiently filter a pre-approved pool.</p>
  
  <p>the "target school" list is a procurement shortcut. recruiters have limited time, so they visit campuses where they have hired before, where alumni vouch for the pipeline, where the brand signals low risk. if your school is not on that list, you are not rejected. you are simply not seen. this is not malice, it is logistics, and it is why <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">new grads are now barely 7% of hires at major tech firms</a>, down from a already-shrunken baseline.</p>
  
  <p>the deeper problem: most career centers teach you to optimize for the system that excludes you. polish your resume for the applicant tracking system. practice elevator pitches for the career fair booth. network with alumni from your school who, statistically, work at the same subset of employers already visiting campus. you are being coached to compete inside a funnel you were never invited to enter.</p>
  
  <p>the honest truth is that companies do not want your school's brand. they want reduced uncertainty about your ability to perform. campus recruiting exists because it is cheaper than evaluating unknown candidates individually. if you can deliver that certainty through a different channel, the campus origin becomes irrelevant.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how do i get on the radar of companies that skip my school entirely</h2>
  
  <p>recruiters live in two places: linkedin search and internal referral networks. your job is to appear in both with evidence, not credentials.</p>
  
  <p>start with linkedin, but not the way career centers teach it. do not list "seeking opportunities" in your headline. do not post motivational content about grinding. instead, make your profile searchable by the skills those companies actually need. if you want product management at a fintech, your headline should say something like "built payment flow prototypes, measured conversion impact." then back it with a project link, a short writeup, and ideally a score or metric.</p>
  
  <p>cold outreach works when it is specific and evidence-based, not when it asks for help. the worst message is "i admire your company, would love to learn about your journey." the better message is: "i rebuilt your onboarding flow as a practice project, cut the steps from 7 to 4, here is the loom walkthrough. does the logic hold?" this gives the recipient something to react to. it also filters for the right people: busy professionals engage with concrete work, not credential begging.</p>
  
  <p>referrals are the hidden hiring channel, and they do not require your school to be on any list. the trick is earning them before you need them. contribute to open-source projects in your target space. answer technical questions in public forums where employees of those companies participate. build small tools that solve problems they have, then share them in communities where those engineers hang out. when you later ask "would you mind passing my portfolio to the hiring manager," it is a favor between people who have already interacted, not a stranger transaction.</p>
  
  <p>the <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">shift to skills-based hiring</a> is your structural opening. 85% of employers now use it, which means the resume screen is increasingly a skills screen. the companies you want are already trying to look past pedigree. your task is to make that easy for them.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what kind of proof actually replaces the target-school signal</h2>
  
  <p>a degree is a mortgage on a depreciating asset: you borrow to buy it, then pay for years, while its value as a hiring signal erodes. what appreciates is proof of work that recruiters can verify in seconds.</p>
  
  <p>the hierarchy of proof, from weakest to strongest:</p>
  
  <p><strong>certificates and coursework.</strong> these signal you showed up. they do not signal you can ship under constraints. a coursera certificate in data analytics tells a recruiter you completed videos. <a href="https://www.coursereport.com/coding-bootcamp-ultimate-guide">coding bootcamps</a> cost ~$13,584 on average and produce audited outcomes through <a href="https://www.cirr.org/schooldata">cirr</a>, but even those are still certificates until paired with projects.</p>
  
  <p><strong>projects with context.</strong> a github repo is better, but only if it includes what problem you solved, what you measured, and what you would do differently. most student repos are code dumps with no narrative. the recruiter has to reconstruct the story, and they will not bother.</p>
  
  <p><strong>scored, comparable work.</strong> the strongest signal is work evaluated against a professional standard, with feedback you have iterated on. this is what zero builds: company-shaped scenarios where your decisions are scored, you see where you land against a professional bar, and you revise. the output is not a badge. it is a record of demonstrated judgment that a recruiter can read in under a minute.</p>
  
  <p>the key distinction: a certificate says you consumed content, while scored proof says you made decisions under uncertainty and showed your reasoning. recruiters hire for the second one because it predicts job performance, and the first one only predicts that you can finish what you start, which is table stakes, not differentiation.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how do i find internships when my career center has no relationships</h2>
  
  <p>internships are the single highest-leverage move for non-target students. <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">they cut underemployment odds by 48.5%</a>, and <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">63.1% convert to full-time</a>. but the postings your career center has are the ones every target-school student already saw.</p>
  
  <p>source differently. company career pages often list internships before aggregators do. set up direct alerts on the jobs pages of your target companies, not just indeed or linkedin. many roles, especially in tech and finance, never hit public boards. they are filled from internal pipelines, employee referrals, or niche communities.</p>
  
  <p>consider the "adjacent internship." if you want google and cannot get google, aim for the startup whose founder left google, or the vendor google uses for a specific function, or the research lab whose alumni rotate into google. the path is not linear. your first internship is a credential for your second, and the company name matters less than the work quality.</p>
  
  <p>finally, build your own internship. contract work, freelance projects, and open-source contributions all count if you document them properly. a student who spent a summer rebuilding a nonprofit's donation flow, measured the conversion lift, and wrote the case study has stronger evidence than a student who alphabetized files at a brand-name bank. recruiters know which internships matter and which are credential theater.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>when should i stop trying to fix my school's system and build my own</h2>
  
  <p>immediately. your school's career infrastructure was built for a different era, when companies recruited geographically and degrees were scarce enough to signal quality. neither is true now.</p>
  
  <p>the <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">burning glass institute found</a> that 46% of employers dropped degree requirements for some roles, but only 31% followed through with real change, and fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected it. this means the rhetoric is ahead of the practice, but also that the practice is shifting. companies are experimenting with how to hire without pedigree. your job is to be the experiment that works.</p>
  
  <p>building your own system means three commitments. first, you stop measuring yourself against your classmates and start measuring against the professional bar in your target field. second, you publish work in public, consistently, so recruiters can find you without your school's intervention. third, you treat every application as a signal-building exercise, not a lottery ticket: if you are rejected, you should have a piece of work or a relationship that outlasts that rejection.</p>
  
  <p>zero is built for this exact path. company-shaped scenarios let you build scored proof that matches what those employers actually evaluate. per-submission feedback replaces the career advisor who has never worked in your target role. and the scoring is designed to be discoverable by recruiters searching for demonstrated skill, not demonstrated school loyalty.</p>
  
  <p>the old path was: attend target school, get recruited, hope for offer. the new path is: build proof, get found, get matched. your school's absence from the recruiting circuit is not a handicap. it is a forcing function to build the better system.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
  
  <h3>will companies really look at candidates from non-target schools if the work is good</h3>
  <p>yes, but the burden of proof is higher. target-school candidates get the benefit of the doubt. you need to eliminate the doubt entirely. that means scored, specific, verifiable work, not just a polished resume. the <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers using skills-based hiring</a> are actively trying to find you, but only if your skills are demonstrable outside your transcript.</p>
  
  <h3>how early should i start building proof if i am a freshman or sophomore</h3>
  <p>now. the students who struggle most are the ones who wait until recruiting season to realize they have no evidence. build projects in your first year, document them, get feedback, iterate. by junior year you should have a portfolio that answers questions before they are asked. the <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">63.1% internship conversion rate</a> is only available to students who have something to show when they apply.</p>
  
  <h3>is it worth transferring to a target school just for recruiting access</h3>
  <p>sometimes, but do the math honestly. the "real product of a tier-1 university is the people," and you are paying roughly $10k per close friend for that network. if you can build equivalent professional relationships through projects, open-source, and intentional cold outreach, you may get the same outcomes without the debt. the status signal is real for some paths, especially finance and consulting, but it is diminishing in tech and most product roles.</p>
  
  <h3>what if i do not know which companies i want yet</h3>
  <p>explore through simulation, not job boards. reading company descriptions is consumption. doing scenario-based projects in different domains is discovery. you learn what you actually enjoy by making decisions and seeing the consequences, not by imagining them. zero's company-shaped scenarios are designed for this: try product, try data, try operations, and let the work tell you where you fit.</p>
  
  <h3>how do i explain my non-target background in interviews without sounding defensive</h3>
  <p>never explain, demonstrate. if asked, say: "i built my path differently, here is what i shipped." then walk through a specific project, the constraints, your decisions, and the outcome. the interviewer will either respect the initiative or reveal a culture that only values pedigree, in which case you have learned something valuable about whether you want to work there.</p>
</section>

